<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Robert Emmet</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1778">1778</a>–<a href="/years/1803">1803</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Robert Emmet was an Irish republican, orator and rebel leader. After the suppression of the United Irish uprising of 1798 he organised a renewed attempt to overthrow British rule and the Protestant Ascendancy in Ireland and to break the recent union with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-great-britain">Great Britain</a>. Hopes of French help faded, many veterans of 1798 held back, and his rising in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin">Dublin</a> in 1803 failed. He was hanged on 20 September 1803. His proclamation of a provisional government, his speech from the dock and his end on the gallows inspired later Irish republicans, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/patrick-pearse">Patrick Pearse</a> in 1916.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/robert-emmet.jpg" alt="Robert Emmet" width="330" height="388" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: John Comerford · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:RobertEmmetWatercolor.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Robert Emmet was born on 4 March 1778 at 109 St Stephen's Green in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin">Dublin</a>, the youngest son of the physician to the Lord Lieutenant and a member of a comfortable Protestant Ascendancy family. His father supported American independence, and his elder brother Thomas Addis Emmet was a friend of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theobald-wolfe-tone">Theobald Wolfe Tone</a>. He entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trinity-college-dublin">Trinity College Dublin</a> in October 1793 at fifteen and excelled in history and chemistry. In April 1798 he was exposed as secretary of a secret college committee backing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/society-of-united-irishmen">Society of United Irishmen</a> and withdrew from Trinity rather than be questioned under oath.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Emmet took no part in the uprising of May 1798, but afterwards he joined William Putnam McCabe in rebuilding the United Irish organisation on a military basis, hoping again for French help. In 1800 he went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> as secretary to Malachy Delaney and, with Delaney, presented <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> with a memorial arguing that the Union had not eased Ireland's discontent. Back in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin">Dublin</a> from October 1802, he prepared a rising with a legacy of £2,000 from his father and the help of his housekeeper Anne Devlin, having come to doubt that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> would put Ireland's interests first.</p><p>A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gunpowder">gunpowder</a> explosion at the rebels' depot in Patrick Street on 16 July 1803 forced his hand, and he brought the date forward to 23 July. His proclamation of a provisional government called on the Irish to rely on their own strength, and announced the abolition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tithe">tithes</a> and the nationalisation of the land of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-ireland">Church of Ireland</a>. Many veterans of 1798 stayed away. The plan to storm <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin-castle">Dublin Castle</a> was botched; by evening he had only about 80 men outside his Thomas Street arsenal and told them to disperse. In the night's clashes the Lord Chief Justice, Lord Kilwarden, was dragged from his carriage and killed with pikes.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Emmet was captured on 25 August, tried for high treason on 19 September and convicted, offering no defence. His Speech from the Dock, of which over seventy versions exist, ends by asking that no man vindicate or calumniate his character until other times and other men could do it justice. He was hanged on 20 September 1803, and his burial place is unknown. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daniel-oconnell">Daniel O'Connell</a> condemned the resort to physical force, but the Young Irelanders began his political rehabilitation in the 1840s. Thomas Moore's ballads made him an icon, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/w-b-yeats">W. B. Yeats</a> observing in 1904 that Emmet died and became an image, and in 1916 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/patrick-pearse">Patrick Pearse</a> again proclaimed a provisional government in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin">Dublin</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Robert Emmet” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In &quot;Green on my Cape&quot;, published in Dundalk in 1802, it is <b>Robert Emmet</b> who meets the narrator, at Brest, and versions of the 1840s and 1850s bring in Napoleon.</blockquote><a href="/songs/the-wearing-of-the-green">The Wearing of the Green</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin">Dublin</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/ireland">Ireland</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin">Dublin</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/ireland">Ireland</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=53.35,-6.26&amp;year=1794" aria-label="Dublin on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Robert Emmet"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4681 5552h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4681 5552h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=53.35,-6.26&amp;year=1794">What was here in 1794? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Let no man write my epitaph; for as no man who knows my motives dare now vindicate them, let not prejudice or ignorance asperse them.&#8221;</p><footer>Speech from the dock, Dublin, 19 September 1803 &#183; original &#183; as printed in Speeches from the Dock (1867) &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/13112/pg13112-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;When my country takes her place among the nations of the earth, then and not till then, let my epitaph be written.&#8221;</p><footer>Speech from the dock, Dublin, 19 September 1803, closing words &#183; original &#183; as printed in Speeches from the Dock (1867) &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/13112/pg13112-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;I am going to my cold and silent grave—my lamp of life is nearly extinguished—my race is run—the grave opens to receive me, and I sink into its bosom.&#8221;</p><footer>Speech from the dock, Dublin, 19 September 1803 &#183; original &#183; as printed in Speeches from the Dock (1867) &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/13112/pg13112-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
